# Cline Rules for Git Repository

This file contains project-specific rules and patterns for working with the Git repository.

## Repository Structure

- The Git codebase is primarily written in C with some shell and Perl scripts
- Built-in commands are implemented in C files in the `builtin/` directory
- Documentation is written in AsciiDoc format in the `Documentation/` directory
- Tests are located in the `t/` directory and are primarily shell scripts

## Contribution Workflow

- Contributions are submitted as patches via email, not pull requests
- Each logical change should be a separate commit with a clear commit message
- Commit messages should explain the "why" not just the "what"
- All changes should include tests and documentation updates
- Patches are reviewed on the mailing list before being accepted

## Coding Standards

- C code follows specific style guidelines outlined in Documentation/CodingGuidelines
- Tabs are used for indentation (8 spaces wide)
- Line length should be kept to 80 characters when possible
- Shell scripts should be POSIX-compatible
- Error messages follow specific formatting rules

## Testing Requirements

- All changes must pass the existing test suite
- New features should include new tests
- Bug fixes should include tests that would have caught the bug

## Documentation Practices

- User-facing documentation is written in AsciiDoc
- Documentation should be updated alongside code changes
- Man pages follow a specific format with standard sections

## Memory Management

- Memory allocation and deallocation should be carefully managed
- Use Git's API for common operations rather than reinventing functionality
